// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["v-a"] = {"qs": [["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "A scheme with ₹1,000 crore of assets has a TER of 1.5% a year. Roughly how much expense is accrued each day (365-day year)?", ["About ₹1.50 crore", "About ₹41.1 lakh", "About ₹4.11 lakh", "About ₹12.50 lakh"], 2, "Annual expense = 1,000 crore × 1.5% = ₹15 crore. Daily accrual = 15 crore / 365 ≈ ₹4.11 lakh. Expenses are accrued daily, so the NAV is always shown net of them."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Stock exchange platforms for mutual fund transactions, such as BSE StAR MF and NSE's NMF platform, allow:", ["Only institutional investors to buy closed-ended schemes", "AMCs to borrow money from each other", "Brokers and distributors to process purchases and redemptions for clients", "Investors to trade units with each other at negotiated prices"], 2, "These exchange platforms provide order routing for subscription and redemption of open-ended schemes, in demat or statement-of-account form, through brokers and mutual fund distributors."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "Why are rolling returns considered more reliable than a single point-to-point return?", ["They always show higher returns than point-to-point figures", "They reduce the effect of the chosen start and end dates", "They are calculated only during bull markets", "They exclude the impact of the expense ratio"], 1, "Point-to-point returns depend heavily on the chosen dates. Rolling returns average performance over many overlapping periods, giving a better picture of consistency."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "When recommending schemes, a mutual fund distributor is required to disclose to the investor:", ["The commissions it receives from the AMCs for the schemes recommended", "Its personal income tax returns and bank statements for the last three financial years", "The fund manager's salary details", "A guarantee of the scheme's future performance"], 0, "SEBI requires distributors to disclose all commissions, such as trail payments, received from competing schemes they recommend. This helps investors see any conflicts of interest."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "MF Utility (MFU) is best described as:", ["A shared platform to transact across many fund houses", "A regulatory body under SEBI that approves new fund offers before they are launched", "A credit rating agency for debt schemes", "An NBFC that lends against mutual fund units"], 0, "MFU is an industry-backed shared services platform that lets investors and distributors transact in schemes of many participating AMCs through a single account number and form."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "The most suitable benchmark for a liquid fund is:", ["The Nifty 50 Total Return Index", "A 10-year government bond index", "A liquid or money market debt index", "The price of gold in rupees"], 2, "A liquid fund invests in very short-term debt, so its benchmark should be a liquid or money market index with similar maturity and credit profile."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which asset is generally the least liquid for an individual investor?", ["Units of a liquid fund", "Shares of a large listed company", "A residential flat", "A savings bank deposit"], 2, "Selling property takes time, involves negotiation, legal work and transaction costs, and may need a price cut. Liquid fund units, large-cap shares and savings deposits can be converted to cash within a day or two."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "A moderate-risk investor needs the money in about three years for a home down payment. Which choice is most appropriate?", ["A small cap fund for higher growth", "A short duration debt fund or conservative hybrid fund", "A sector fund that has done well recently", "A 10-year gilt fund with constant maturity"], 1, "A three-year goal with moderate risk calls for limited equity and moderate interest rate risk. Small cap and sectoral funds are too volatile, and a 10-year gilt fund carries high duration risk."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Which is a limitation of investing through a mutual fund compared with holding shares directly?", ["The investor gets no professional management at all", "The investor cannot tailor the portfolio to personal preferences", "The investor cannot redeem units in an open-ended scheme", "The investor must buy at least one full share of each company"], 1, "A scheme follows its stated mandate for all investors, so individual choices on specific stocks cannot be accommodated. Professional management and liquidity are benefits, not limitations."], ["Scheme Related Information", "A scheme's annual report (or its abridged version) must be sent or made available to unitholders within:", ["Four months from the end of the financial year", "Two weeks from the end of the financial year", "Twelve months from the end of the financial year", "One week after the scheme's NFO closes"], 0, "SEBI requires mutual funds to publish annual reports, and make abridged reports available to unitholders, within four months of the close of the financial year."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "A scheme can be wound up if unitholders pass a resolution to that effect. What majority is required?", ["Three-fourths (75%) of the unitholders", "A simple majority of more than 50%", "Two-thirds of the trustees only", "Ninety per cent of the AMC's directors"], 0, "Regulations allow a scheme to be wound up if 75% of its unitholders pass a resolution, or when the trustees decide so for stated reasons, or when SEBI directs it in the interest of unitholders."], ["Taxation", "Short-term capital gains on units of an equity-oriented fund, sold on or after 23 July 2024, are taxed at:", ["20%", "15%", "10%", "The investor's slab rate"], 0, "Since 23 July 2024, STCG on equity-oriented fund units (held 12 months or less) on which STT is paid is taxed at 20%, up from the earlier 15%."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Under SEBI categorisation, an AMC may offer:", ["Both a value fund and a contra fund at any time", "A contra fund only if it also runs a value fund", "Neither, as these categories were withdrawn", "Either a value fund or a contra fund, but not both"], 3, "SEBI permits one scheme per category. Value and contra funds are treated as alternatives within one slot, so a fund house may offer only one of the two."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "Unclaimed redemption and IDCW amounts lying with a mutual fund are:", ["Transferred to the AMC's own profit and loss account after one full year of remaining unclaimed", "Kept idle in a current account without any return", "Invested in special plans of liquid or money market schemes", "Distributed among the other unitholders of the scheme"], 2, "SEBI requires unclaimed amounts to be invested in specially created plans of liquid, overnight or money market schemes, so they keep earning returns until claimed by the rightful investors."], ["Taxation", "Long-term capital gains on equity-oriented fund units are currently taxed at:", ["10% on gains above ₹1 lakh in a financial year", "20% with indexation benefit", "12.5% on gains above ₹1.25 lakh in a financial year", "Nil, as long-term equity gains are exempt"], 2, "From 23 July 2024, LTCG on equity-oriented funds is taxed at 12.5% on gains exceeding ₹1.25 lakh in a year. The earlier rule was 10% on gains above ₹1 lakh."], ["Investor Services", "An investor holds mutual fund units but has made no transaction for several months. How often will a Consolidated Account Statement still be sent?", ["Only when the investor asks for it in writing", "Every week, by email only", "Never, until a new transaction is made", "Every half-year"], 3, "Investors who transact receive a CAS for that month. Those without any transaction still receive a CAS every half-year showing holdings and their value, so records stay up to date."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A fund returned 13% while the risk-free rate was 6%. Its beta is 1.4. What is its Treynor ratio?", ["9.3", "4.3", "5.0", "7.0"], 2, "Treynor ratio = (fund return − risk-free rate) / beta = (13 − 6) / 1.4 = 5.0. It measures excess return per unit of systematic risk, unlike Sharpe, which uses total risk."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "Category average returns can look better than reality because schemes that were merged or closed due to poor performance are dropped from the data. This is called:", ["Recency bias", "Confirmation bias", "Survivorship bias", "Home bias"], 2, "Survivorship bias arises when only surviving funds are counted. Since weak funds tend to be closed or merged, the average of survivors overstates what investors actually earned."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "An investor wants to build an emergency fund that must be accessible within a day, with very low risk. The most suitable scheme is:", ["A liquid fund", "A small cap fund", "A credit risk fund", "A sectoral equity fund"], 0, "Liquid funds invest in instruments maturing within 91 days, have low volatility and process redemptions quickly, which suits emergency money. Equity and credit risk funds are too volatile."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which risk cannot be reduced simply by adding more stocks to an equity portfolio, however many are added?", ["Market (systematic) risk", "Company-specific risk", "Management risk of one firm", "Sector concentration risk"], 0, "Systematic risk comes from factors like interest rates, inflation or recessions that affect the whole market, so diversification within equities cannot remove it. Company and sector risks fall as holdings are spread."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A fund returned 15%, the market returned 12%, the risk-free rate was 6% and the fund's beta is 1.2. What is the fund's Jensen's alpha?", ["1.8%", "3.0%", "1.2%", "0.6%"], 0, "Expected return (CAPM) = 6 + 1.2 × (12 − 6) = 13.2%. Alpha = 15 − 13.2 = 1.8%. Simply comparing with the market (3%) ignores the fund's higher beta."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Which constituent calculates the NAV of a scheme by recording income, expenses and valuations?", ["Custodian", "Fund accountant", "Collecting banker", "Trustee"], 1, "Fund accounting, whether done in-house by the AMC or outsourced, records all transactions and computes the daily NAV. The custodian holds securities and the collecting bank receives investor money."], ["Investor Services", "Units of a new fund offer must be allotted to investors within how many business days of the NFO closing?", ["15 business days", "5 business days", "30 business days", "1 business day"], 1, "SEBI requires allotment of units and dispatch of statements within five business days of the NFO closure, so investors can begin transacting soon after."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "An Execution-Only Platform (EOP) in the mutual fund context is a platform that:", ["Provides personalised investment advice for a fee", "Distributes regular plans and earns commission", "Manages portfolios on a discretionary basis", "Facilitates transactions in direct plans without providing advice"], 3, "SEBI's EOP framework covers platforms that only execute transactions in direct plans. They neither advise nor earn distribution commission, so they cannot be used as disguised advisory or distribution channels."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "An investor holds three different large cap funds. The main drawback of this choice is:", ["High portfolio overlap, giving limited extra diversification", "A higher tax rate that applies once an investor holds more than one large cap fund", "A lock-in on each additional large cap fund", "SEBI's ban on holding more than one large cap fund"], 0, "Large cap funds draw from the same top 100 stocks, so three such funds often hold similar shares. The investor adds complexity and cost without much extra diversification."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "A scheme has investments and accrued income totalling ₹612 crore, liabilities of ₹12 crore and 40 crore units outstanding. Its NAV is:", ["₹15.30", "₹14.70", "₹15.60", "₹15.00"], 3, "NAV = (assets − liabilities) / units = (612 − 12) / 40 = ₹15.00. ₹15.30 ignores the liabilities, and ₹14.70 subtracts them twice."], ["Investor Services", "An investor invests in a liquid fund at 1:00 pm on a Friday; funds are credited to the scheme before the cut-off. Monday is a business day. Which NAV applies?", ["NAV of Friday", "NAV of Monday", "NAV of Thursday", "NAV of Sunday, the day before the next business day"], 3, "For liquid and overnight funds, an application before 1:30 pm with funds realised gets the NAV of the day immediately preceding the next business day. Here that is Sunday, so the investor earns weekend returns."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Which of the following activities is an AMC NOT permitted to undertake?", ["Managing portfolios under PMS through a separate business unit", "Offering advisory services to pooled funds, subject to SEBI conditions", "Acting as a trustee of any mutual fund", "Managing its own schemes' investment portfolios"], 2, "SEBI regulations bar an AMC from acting as trustee of any mutual fund. AMCs may undertake certain other permitted businesses, such as PMS or advisory services, with safeguards against conflicts of interest."], ["Investment Landscape", "A planner temporarily raises equity from the long-term 60% to 70% because valuations look attractive, planning to return to 60% later. This is:", ["Strategic asset allocation", "Rupee cost averaging", "Tactical asset allocation", "Portfolio rebalancing to target"], 2, "Tactical allocation is a short-term deviation from the strategic mix to exploit market conditions. Strategic allocation is the long-term mix based on goals and risk profile, which the planner intends to restore."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "How are most debt securities held by mutual fund schemes valued for NAV purposes?", ["At the price paid by the scheme, until maturity of the bond", "Using prices from independent valuation agencies", "At face value, irrespective of the market conditions", "At a price decided by the fund manager each day"], 1, "To ensure fair and consistent NAVs, debt securities are valued using prices provided by independent valuation agencies. Holding at cost or face value would hide market movements."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "An investor holds 2,000 units under the IDCW reinvestment option. The scheme declares IDCW of ₹1.20 per unit and the ex-IDCW NAV is ₹24. Ignoring tax, how many additional units are allotted?", ["100 units", "120 units", "96 units", "48 units"], 0, "Amount reinvested = 2,000 × 1.20 = ₹2,400. Units allotted = 2,400 / 24 = 100. Reinvestment uses the ex-IDCW NAV, so total investment value is unchanged."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "CAMS and KFin Technologies are examples of which type of mutual fund service provider?", ["Custodians", "Registrar and Transfer Agents", "Credit rating agencies", "Stock exchanges"], 1, "RTAs process purchases, redemptions and switches, maintain unitholder records and send statements on behalf of AMCs. CAMS and KFin Technologies are the major RTAs in the Indian mutual fund industry."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "Which measure best indicates how sensitive a debt fund's NAV is to a change in interest rates?", ["Standard deviation of equity returns", "Modified duration", "Credit rating of the portfolio", "Expense ratio of the scheme"], 1, "Modified duration estimates the percentage change in price for a 1% change in yield. A higher modified duration means larger NAV swings when interest rates move."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which of the following is a financial asset?", ["Gold jewellery kept in a locker at home", "Agricultural land held for farming", "A commercial shop let out on rent", "Units of a gold exchange traded fund"], 3, "A gold ETF unit is a claim on a fund's assets, which makes it a financial asset even though the fund holds gold. Jewellery, land and property are real (physical) assets."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Product labelling of a mutual fund scheme mainly informs the investor about:", ["The fund manager's personal investment record and qualifications over the last ten years", "The scheme's nature and its risk level on the riskometer", "The guaranteed return for the next year", "The commission structure for distributors"], 1, "Product labels describe the scheme's objective and type of investment in simple words and show a riskometer, helping investors judge whether the scheme suits them."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "The Sortino ratio differs from the Sharpe ratio because it uses:", ["Beta instead of standard deviation", "The benchmark return instead of the risk-free rate", "Gross return before deducting expenses", "Only downside deviation as the measure of risk"], 3, "Sortino penalises only harmful volatility, using downside deviation below a target return. Sharpe uses total standard deviation, which also counts upside volatility as risk."], ["Taxation", "An investor switches from the regular plan to the direct plan of the same scheme. For tax purposes, this switch is:", ["Ignored, as the scheme remains the same", "A redemption plus a fresh purchase, so gains may be taxed", "Treated as a gift between plans", "Tax-exempt only for equity schemes"], 1, "Regular and direct plans are separate plans with different NAVs, so a switch is a sale of units in one plan and a purchase in another. Any gain is taxable and exit load may apply."], ["Investor Services", "The sole holder of a folio has died and a nominee is registered. To get the units, the nominee should normally submit:", ["A transmission request with the death certificate and the nominee's KYC", "A fresh NFO application in the deceased's name", "A court succession certificate and indemnity bond in every case, regardless of nominee", "A redemption request signed by the deceased"], 0, "Where a valid nominee exists, transmission is simple: the nominee submits the transmission form, death certificate and own KYC. Succession certificates are needed only when there is no nominee and amounts are large."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "A cost-conscious investor who believes most active managers will not beat the market over time would prefer:", ["A thematic fund focused on one theme", "A fund with the highest past one-year return", "An index fund or ETF tracking a broad index", "A focused fund holding 25 stocks"], 2, "Passive funds track an index at low cost, avoiding the risk of a manager underperforming. Chasing last year's top performer or thematic bets adds manager and concentration risk."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "SEBI's limits on the Total Expense Ratio of open-ended schemes are structured so that:", ["Every scheme can charge the same flat 2.5% expense ratio", "The maximum permitted TER falls as a scheme's AUM grows", "TER rises as AUM grows, to reward large schemes", "TER can be decided freely by the AMC each month"], 1, "TER limits are slab-based: higher AUM slabs carry lower maximum percentages, so investors benefit from economies of scale as schemes grow."], ["Investor Services", "An investor puts ₹2,000 a month through a SIP for three months at NAVs of ₹16, ₹20 and ₹25. What is the average cost per unit (to two decimals)?", ["₹20.33", "₹19.67", "₹20.00", "₹19.25"], 1, "Units bought = 125 + 100 + 80 = 305. Average cost = 6,000 / 305 = ₹19.67, below the simple average NAV of ₹20.33 because more units are bought when NAV is low."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "Credit risk in a debt fund is best gauged by looking at:", ["The scheme's modified duration", "The number of investors in the scheme", "The credit ratings of the securities in the portfolio", "The scheme's beta relative to Nifty 50"], 2, "Credit ratings indicate the likelihood of issuers paying interest and principal on time. Lower-rated holdings mean higher credit risk; duration measures interest rate risk instead."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A distributor's NISM certificate is about to expire. To keep the ARN valid, the distributor can:", ["Simply pay the renewal fee to AMFI and continue, without any further step", "Re-take the exam or complete the prescribed CPE", "Ask any AMC to extend the certificate", "Continue selling for two more years without action"], 1, "ARN renewal requires a valid NISM certification, which can be renewed by re-taking the exam or attending the prescribed CPE programme before expiry."], ["Investor Services", "When a minor unitholder attains majority, what must happen to the folio?", ["The units are automatically redeemed and paid to the guardian", "The guardian continues to operate it for five more years", "It must be changed to major status; until then it is frozen", "Nothing changes; the guardian keeps operating the folio"], 2, "On attaining majority, the investor must submit the change-of-status request with own KYC and bank details. Until then the folio is frozen for transactions, and SIPs registered by the guardian stop."], ["Investment Landscape", "A retired person drawing regular income from accumulated savings, with little new saving, is in which stage of the wealth cycle?", ["Accumulation stage", "Transition stage", "Inter-generational transfer stage", "Distribution (reaping) stage"], 3, "In the distribution stage the investor lives off the corpus built earlier. Accumulation is the earning-and-saving phase, and transition is the period just before retirement when goals come close."], ["Investment Landscape", "An investor buys a small cap fund only because colleagues and relatives have recently bought it. This behaviour is best described as:", ["Herd mentality", "Anchoring", "Loss aversion", "Mental accounting"], 0, "Herding means following what others do rather than independent analysis. Anchoring is over-reliance on a reference number, and loss aversion is feeling losses more than equivalent gains."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "An investor who wants guidance on scheme selection and periodic review from a mutual fund distributor will typically invest in:", ["The direct plan of the scheme", "A closed-ended plan only", "The regular plan of the scheme", "An ETF through the AMC office"], 2, "Distributors are paid through trail commission included in the regular plan's TER. Investors who want a distributor's service therefore invest in the regular plan; direct plans carry no commission."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "An investor redeems 1,000 units at an NAV of ₹50 when a 1% exit load applies. The redemption proceeds (before taxes) are:", ["₹49,500", "₹50,000", "₹50,500", "₹49,000"], 0, "Redemption price = NAV × (1 − exit load) = 50 × 0.99 = ₹49.50. For 1,000 units the proceeds are ₹49,500. Exit load reduces, never increases, the amount paid."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A credit risk fund must invest at least 65% of its assets in:", ["Corporate bonds rated AA and below, excluding AA+", "AAA-rated public sector bonds only", "Government securities of any maturity", "Money market instruments maturing within 91 days"], 0, "Credit risk funds seek extra yield by taking lower-rated paper: at least 65% in corporate bonds rated AA and below (excluding AA+). This raises both potential return and default risk."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "SEBI derives its statutory powers to regulate mutual funds primarily from the:", ["Companies Act, 2013", "Indian Trusts Act, 1882", "Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934", "SEBI Act, 1992"], 3, "The SEBI Act, 1992 established SEBI and gave it powers to regulate collective investment vehicles including mutual funds. The SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996 are framed under this Act."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "An investor who invests in a mutual fund through a SEBI-registered investment adviser (who charges a fee) will normally be allotted units of the:", ["Direct plan", "Regular plan", "IDCW plan", "Institutional plan"], 0, "RIAs are paid fees by clients and do not earn commissions, so their clients invest in direct plans, which carry lower expenses. Regular plans include distributor commission."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "A fund's NAV rose from ₹10 to ₹13 in six months. What is its simple annualised return?", ["30%", "69%", "15%", "60%"], 3, "Absolute return = 3/10 = 30% over six months. Simple annualised return = 30% × (12/6) = 60%. The compounded annualised figure would be 69%, which is a different measure."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "A very large AUM can be a concern mainly for which type of scheme?", ["A liquid fund", "A small cap fund", "A Nifty 50 index fund", "An overnight fund"], 1, "Small cap stocks have limited liquidity. A very large small cap fund may struggle to buy or sell without moving prices, which can hurt returns. Large-cap index funds and liquid funds handle size more easily."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Changes to an SID between its periodic updates are communicated through:", ["A fresh new fund offer", "A Consolidated Account Statement", "A letter to SEBI only", "An addendum to the document"], 3, "When information in the SID changes, the AMC issues an addendum that is published and becomes part of the SID until the document is fully updated."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Under current SEBI norms, mutual fund distributors are paid:", ["Only upfront commission at the time of purchase", "A fixed fee directly by SEBI", "Only trail-based commission, with no upfront commission", "A share of the scheme's capital gains"], 2, "SEBI requires that all commissions be paid as trail from the scheme's expenses, without upfront payments. This reduces the incentive for churning investors across schemes."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which change would NOT be treated as a change in a scheme's fundamental attributes?", ["Change in the type of scheme from open-ended to closed-ended", "Change in the scheme's investment objective", "Replacement of the scheme's fund manager", "Change in the terms of issue, such as fees and loads affecting investors"], 2, "Fundamental attributes include the type of scheme, investment objective and terms of issue. A change of fund manager is disclosed, but it does not require giving unitholders an exit option without load."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Retirement and children's funds (solution-oriented schemes) carry a lock-in of:", ["Exactly 3 years from the date of each individual investment", "No lock-in, but an exit load of 1%", "10 years for all investors", "At least 5 years, or till retirement or majority if earlier"], 3, "Solution-oriented schemes have a lock-in of at least 5 years or until the retirement age or the child attaining majority, whichever is earlier, to encourage goal-based investing."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "A mutual fund may borrow money:", ["Freely and for any tenure, to increase the scheme's exposure to equity markets beyond its net assets", "Only from its sponsor, without any limit or tenure", "Never, as borrowing of any kind is fully prohibited", "Only for temporary liquidity needs, up to 20% of net assets for 6 months"], 3, "Borrowing is allowed only for temporary liquidity needs, such as redemptions or IDCW payouts, and is capped at 20% of net assets for a maximum of six months. Leveraging to buy securities is not permitted."], ["Taxation", "For a resident investor, IDCW received from a mutual fund is:", ["Added to income and taxed at the investor's applicable slab rate", "Fully tax-free in the investor's hands", "Taxed at a flat 10% irrespective of the investor's total income for the year", "Taxed only when units are finally redeemed"], 0, "Since the abolition of dividend distribution tax in 2020, IDCW is taxable in the hands of the investor at slab rates, with TDS deducted by the fund above the prescribed threshold."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "Which statement about mutual fund advertisements is correct?", ["They may promise a minimum return for equity schemes if the AMC has a long and consistent track record", "They must carry the standard risk warning and show performance against the benchmark", "They may use performance of only the best year", "They do not need any disclosure if shown online"], 1, "SEBI's advertisement code requires the standard warning about market risks and, where performance is shown, comparison with the benchmark over standard periods. Selective or assured-return claims are not allowed."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "Excluding index and sector-specific schemes, a mutual fund scheme cannot invest more than what share of its NAV in the equity shares of a single company?", ["10%", "5%", "15%", "25%"], 0, "SEBI caps investment in equity of any one company at 10% of a scheme's NAV, to ensure diversification. Index funds and ETFs may follow index weights, and sectoral funds have separate treatment."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which type of scheme is required to disclose its portfolio every fortnight, in addition to monthly disclosure?", ["Equity schemes", "Index funds tracking Nifty", "Debt schemes", "ELSS schemes"], 2, "To improve transparency on credit and liquidity risk, SEBI requires debt schemes to disclose portfolios fortnightly. Other schemes disclose portfolios monthly and half-yearly."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Under the traditional eligibility route, a sponsor must contribute at least what share of the AMC's net worth?", ["26%", "51%", "10%", "40%"], 3, "SEBI's traditional sponsor eligibility requires the sponsor to contribute at least 40% of the AMC's net worth, along with conditions on track record, profitability and net worth of the sponsor."], ["Taxation", "For equity fund units bought before 1 February 2018 and sold later, the cost of acquisition for computing LTCG is:", ["Always the actual purchase cost paid, without considering any market value adjustment", "The NAV on 1 April 2001", "Higher of actual cost and 31 Jan 2018 FMV (capped at sale value)", "Zero, since gains up to 2018 are taxable"], 2, "Under grandfathering, gains accrued up to 31 January 2018 are protected. Cost is taken as the higher of actual cost and the lower of FMV on 31 January 2018 and the sale value."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "The 'maximum drawdown' of a fund refers to:", ["The highest NAV the fund has ever reached", "The largest fall in value from a peak to a subsequent trough", "The largest single-day redemption by investors", "The maximum exit load the fund may charge"], 1, "Maximum drawdown measures the worst peak-to-trough decline over a period. It shows how much an investor could have lost by buying at the top, which standard deviation alone does not reveal."], ["Investor Services", "Small SIP investments up to ₹50,000 a year per investor per fund house can be made without PAN through:", ["A cash deposit with any bank branch", "A simple self-declaration of income on the form", "PAN-exempt KYC (PEKRN) registration under the micro investment route", "The investor's Aadhaar number printed on the cheque"], 2, "Micro investments up to ₹50,000 a year (including micro SIPs) are exempt from PAN if the investor completes PAN-exempt KYC and obtains a PEKRN. Other KYC norms continue to apply."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which SEBI-prescribed document presents key scheme information in a standard, machine-readable format on AMC and AMFI websites?", ["Statement of Additional Information", "Scheme Summary Document", "Key Information Memorandum", "Consolidated Account Statement"], 1, "The Scheme Summary Document captures key data points of every scheme, such as fund manager, benchmark, loads and AUM, in a standard machine-readable format, making comparison across schemes easier."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "An index fund returned 11.4% in a year while its benchmark TRI returned 12.0%. The tracking difference is:", ["−0.6%", "+0.6%", "−1.2%", "23.4%"], 0, "Tracking difference = fund return − index return = 11.4 − 12.0 = −0.6%. It largely reflects expenses and cash drag. Tracking error is a different measure: the variability of these differences."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "When an AMC presents a scheme's past performance in its communications, it must show it:", ["Only for the period in which returns were highest", "Alongside its benchmark's returns for the same periods", "Without any comparison, to avoid confusing investors", "Along with a promise of similar future returns"], 1, "SEBI requires performance to be shown against the benchmark (and an additional benchmark where specified) for standard periods, so investors can judge the scheme's value addition."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "For a debt scheme that holds bonds to maturity, a good indicator of the likely return is:", ["The scheme's highest one-day return", "The past one-month return annualised", "The number of bonds in the portfolio", "The portfolio yield to maturity minus the expense ratio"], 3, "If securities are held to maturity, investors broadly earn the portfolio YTM, less expenses. Short-term past returns can be distorted by price changes from rate movements."], ["Scheme Related Information", "The Potential Risk Class (PRC) matrix for debt schemes classifies a scheme on the basis of:", ["Its maximum interest rate risk and maximum credit risk", "Its past three-year returns and expense ratio", "The size of its AUM and number of investors", "The fund manager's experience and tenure"], 0, "The PRC matrix shows the maximum interest rate risk (measured by Macaulay duration) and maximum credit risk (measured by a credit risk value) the scheme may take, so investors know its risk limits in advance."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "A distributor tells a client that a credit risk debt fund is 'as safe as a bank fixed deposit'. This is:", ["Acceptable if the fund has never defaulted", "A misrepresentation that breaches the code of conduct", "Acceptable because both are debt products", "Required to simplify things for the investor"], 1, "Mutual fund schemes are market-linked and carry credit and interest rate risk; they carry no deposit insurance. Comparing a credit risk fund to an FD misleads the investor about risk."], ["Investment Landscape", "₹5,00,000 is invested at 8% a year, compounded annually. What will it be worth after 5 years (nearest rupee)?", ["₹7,00,000", "₹7,34,664", "₹7,20,000", "₹7,93,437"], 1, "Future value = 5,00,000 × (1.08)^5 = ₹7,34,664. ₹7,00,000 is simple interest for 5 years, while ₹7,93,437 is the compounded value after 6 years."], ["Investment Landscape", "A bank deposit earns 9% a year while inflation is 5%. Using the exact (Fisher) method, the real rate of return is closest to:", ["4.00%", "3.81%", "14.45%", "4.20%"], 1, "Real return = (1 + nominal) / (1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.09 / 1.05 − 1 = 3.81%. Simply subtracting gives 4.00%, a rough approximation that slightly overstates the real return."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Before a distributor can sell a particular AMC's schemes and earn commission, the distributor must:", ["Obtain a separate licence from RBI", "Own a minimum number of the AMC's shares", "Invest personally in every scheme sold", "Be empanelled with that AMC"], 3, "Each AMC empanels distributors after checking ARN and other details. Only empanelled distributors receive commission from that AMC for business they bring in."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "An interval scheme is one that:", ["Allows purchase and redemption with the AMC on every single business day of the year", "Never allows redemption until its final maturity", "Allows purchase and redemption only during specified transaction periods", "Pays interest to unitholders at fixed intervals"], 2, "Interval schemes combine features of open and closed-ended schemes. Units can be bought or redeemed only during pre-announced intervals; at other times they are traded on a stock exchange."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A balanced advantage (dynamic asset allocation) fund differs from an aggressive hybrid fund mainly because it:", ["Must always hold between 65% and 80% of its assets in equity instruments", "Invests only in government securities", "Can vary its equity–debt mix dynamically within a wide range", "Is a closed-ended scheme with fixed maturity"], 2, "Balanced advantage funds shift between equity and debt (0–100%) based on a model or view. Aggressive hybrid funds must keep equity between 65% and 80% of assets."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "The document through which the sponsor creates the mutual fund as a trust is the:", ["Trust deed", "Investment Management Agreement", "Scheme Information Document", "Memorandum of Association of the AMC"], 0, "The sponsor executes a trust deed in favour of the trustees, which is registered under the Indian Registration Act. The Investment Management Agreement is a separate contract between trustees and the AMC."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "SEBI requires AMCs to invest a minimum amount in their own schemes, linked to each scheme's risk level. The main purpose is to:", ["Provide the AMC with guaranteed income", "Allow the AMC to vote on behalf of investors", "Align the AMC's interests with those of unitholders", "Increase the scheme's expense ratio"], 2, "This 'skin in the game' rule means the AMC shares in the scheme's outcomes, which encourages responsible management. Higher-risk schemes require larger minimum investments by the AMC."], ["Investor Services", "An NRI wants to invest so that both principal and gains can be taken abroad. The investment should be made through:", ["An NRE account", "An NRO account", "A resident savings account", "A cash payment at the AMC office"], 0, "Investments routed through the NRE account (or FCNR) are repatriable. Investments from an NRO account are generally non-repatriable, subject to permitted annual remittance limits."], ["Risk, Return and Performance of Funds", "Reinvestment risk for a debt investor is highest when:", ["Rates fall and coupons are reinvested at lower yields", "Interest rates are rising steadily and new bonds are being issued at higher coupons", "The bond is a zero-coupon bond held to maturity", "Inflation is falling with stable interest rates"], 0, "Reinvestment risk is the risk that cash flows received are reinvested at lower rates. It bites when rates fall. A zero-coupon bond held to maturity has no interim cash flows, so it has no reinvestment risk."], ["Taxation", "An investor's LTCG on equity fund units in a financial year is ₹2,00,000 and there are no other equity gains. Ignoring cess and surcharge, the tax payable is:", ["₹25,000", "₹9,375", "₹15,000", "₹20,000"], 1, "Taxable gain = 2,00,000 − 1,25,000 exemption = ₹75,000. Tax at 12.5% = ₹9,375. ₹25,000 wrongly applies 12.5% to the whole gain."], ["Legal and Regulatory Framework", "All schemes of a mutual fund taken together cannot own more than what share of any company's paid-up capital carrying voting rights?", ["5%", "20%", "10%", "26%"], 2, "The 10% fund-house-level limit on voting capital prevents a mutual fund from gaining control or undue influence over a company it invests in."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "Why might an investor prefer the growth option with an SWP over the IDCW option for regular cash flow?", ["IDCW payouts are guaranteed by the AMC every month, whereas SWP amounts can never be fixed", "SWP withdrawals are always fully tax-free for every investor", "The IDCW option does not allow redemption at any time", "SWP controls amount and timing; only the gain part is taxed"], 3, "IDCW amounts depend on the AMC's decision and are fully taxable at slab rates. SWP lets the investor fix the amount and date, and tax applies only to the capital gain component."], ["Taxation", "An investor bought units of a debt fund (investing more than 65% in debt) in 2024 and sells them after four years. The gain is:", ["Long-term, taxed at 20% with indexation", "Long-term, taxed at 12.5% without indexation", "Fully exempt after three years", "Treated as short-term and taxed at the investor's slab rate"], 3, "For specified debt-oriented funds bought after 1 April 2023, gains are deemed short-term irrespective of holding period and taxed at slab rates. Indexation benefits do not apply."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A registered mutual fund distributor who is not an RIA wants to print business cards. Which description is appropriate?", ["Independent Financial Adviser", "Mutual Fund Distributor, with the ARN mentioned", "Investment Adviser, SEBI-certified", "Wealth Adviser and Portfolio Manager"], 1, "SEBI and AMFI require distributors to describe themselves as 'Mutual Fund Distributor' and not use terms like 'adviser' that suggest advisory registration. This avoids misleading investors about their role."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Trustees of a mutual fund are required to submit reports on the fund's activities to SEBI:", ["Only when a scheme is wound up", "Every five years", "Only if an investor complains", "Every half-year"], 3, "Trustees must file half-yearly reports with SEBI on the activities of the mutual fund, confirming compliance by the AMC. This is part of their supervisory role over the AMC."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "By what time must the NAV of most open-ended schemes be published on AMC and AMFI websites for each business day?", ["9:00 am on the next business day", "The end of the following week", "11:00 pm on the same business day", "Only at month end"], 2, "SEBI requires NAVs of open-ended schemes to be uploaded on the AMC and AMFI websites by 11 pm each business day (special timelines apply to some schemes such as fund of funds investing overseas)."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Selection", "When choosing between two equity funds in the same category, which factor is the most reliable guide?", ["Which one topped the one-month return chart", "Which one has the newest launch date", "Which one has the lowest NAV per unit", "Consistent performance versus benchmark across cycles"], 3, "Consistent performance over full cycles reflects process, not luck. A low NAV does not make a fund cheaper, and short-term rankings change frequently."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "When comparing a scheme's performance with other schemes, the peer group should consist of:", ["All mutual fund schemes in the industry", "Schemes managed by the same fund manager", "Schemes launched in the same calendar year", "Schemes in the same SEBI category with similar objectives"], 3, "Peer comparison is meaningful only when schemes share objectives, asset allocation and risk, which is why SEBI categories are used. Comparing across categories mixes very different risks."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Under SEBI categorisation, a focused fund may hold a maximum of:", ["20 stocks", "30 stocks", "50 stocks", "100 stocks"], 1, "A focused fund invests in a concentrated portfolio of at most 30 stocks. It must state the market cap focus (large, mid, small or multi) and invest at least 65% in equity."], ["NAV, TER and Pricing of Units", "Which of the following is NOT charged to the scheme within its TER?", ["Fees paid to the custodian", "Trail commission paid to distributors in a regular plan", "Fees paid to the registrar and transfer agent", "Marketing costs of the AMC beyond permitted limits"], 3, "Only permitted expenses within SEBI limits may be charged to the scheme; any excess must be borne by the AMC. Custodian fees, RTA fees and distributor trail are standard scheme expenses."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A Fund of Funds (FoF) scheme primarily invests in:", ["Units of other mutual fund schemes", "Shares of asset management companies", "Bank fixed deposits of different tenors", "Government securities only"], 0, "An FoF builds its portfolio from units of other schemes, domestic or overseas. Investors get diversification across funds but bear an additional layer of expenses."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which of the following is NOT one of the six levels on SEBI's mutual fund riskometer?", ["Low to Moderate", "Moderately High", "Very High", "Moderately Low"], 3, "The six riskometer levels are Low, Low to Moderate, Moderate, Moderately High, High and Very High. 'Moderately Low' is not a riskometer level."], ["Mutual Fund Scheme Performance", "A scheme's NAV grew from ₹10 to ₹20 over exactly 5 years. Its CAGR is closest to:", ["14.87%", "20.00%", "15.00%", "12.25%"], 0, "CAGR = (20/10)^(1/5) − 1 = 14.87%. Dividing the 100% absolute gain by 5 gives 20%, which ignores compounding."], ["Taxation", "A short-term capital loss on mutual fund units can be set off against:", ["Only short-term capital gains", "Salary income in the same year", "Any income, including interest", "Both short-term and long-term capital gains"], 3, "Short-term capital losses can be set off against both STCG and LTCG. Long-term capital losses can be set off only against LTCG. Capital losses cannot be set off against salary."]]};
